\title{Deterministic Wetware Dynamics:\\Universality, Finite-State Recurrence, and Configuration Information}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study an abstract model of wetware computation in which a state evolves by repeated application of a deterministic transition map. Iteration obeys a semigroup law, and every function $f:X\to Y$ admits a one-step dynamical realization when the state space and interfaces may depend on $f$. Every orbit on a finite state space has a periodic tail; recurrence survives arbitrary observation and prevents an observed orbit from enumerating pairwise distinct natural numbers forever. We also compare two finite configuration families. Deterministic transition maps on $n$ states number $n^n$ and require exactly $n\log_2 n$ bits to identify, whereas arbitrary $n\times n$ binary connection matrices number $2^{n^2}$ and require $n^2$ bits. For every $n\geq1$ the first cost is strictly smaller, and their ratio tends to zero. These are description-length results, not claims about thermodynamic energy. Together they expose a structural tradeoff: unconstrained representation gives broad realization power, finite dynamics forces recurrence, and determinism compresses configuration freedom relative to unrestricted connectivity.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Biological computation is naturally described as dynamics. A nervous system has an internal condition, receives encoded input, evolves, and exposes observable output. At a sufficiently abstract level, the central mathematical object is a state-transition system.

We investigate the deterministic model consisting of a state space $S$ and a map $F:S\to S$. Its evolution from $x\in S$ is
\[
x,F(x),F^2(x),\ldots.
\]
Despite its minimality, the model supports exact statements about realization, recurrence, and information.

The first theme is expressive adequacy. Once an encoder and decoder are included, every function $f:X\to Y$ can be represented by a one-step system on the tagged state space $X\sqcup Y$. This must not be confused with uniform simulation of a standard machine: the architecture, transition, and interfaces may all depend on $f$, so resource efficiency is deliberately absent.

The second theme is a finite-state obstruction. Infinite time cannot produce infinitely many distinct states from a finite state space. A collision is unavoidable; because the update is deterministic, the complete future after that collision repeats. Every finite-state orbit and every observation of it are eventually periodic.

The third theme connects counting to information. There are $n^n$ self-maps of an $n$-element set, but $2^{n^2}$ binary matrices of shape $n\times n$. Taking base-$2$ logarithms yields exact configuration-information costs $n\log_2 n$ and $n^2$. The former is strictly lower for every positive integer and asymptotically negligible in ratio.

The term ``wetware'' motivates the comparison, but the results apply to any deterministic finite dynamics. Likewise, ``energy'' below means only the number of bits required to select a configuration. No conclusion about heat, metabolic cost, or engineering efficiency follows without an additional physical model.

\section{Dynamical model and computation}

\begin{definition}[Wetware system]
A wetware system on a state space $S$ is a pair $(S,F)$ in which $F:S\to S$ is a deterministic one-step transition map.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Run]
For $t\in\mathbb N$ and $x\in S$, the state reached after $t$ steps is
\[
R(t,x)=F^t(x),
\]
where $F^0$ is the identity and $F^{t+1}=F\circ F^t$.
\end{definition}

Thus $R(0,x)=x$, $R(1,x)=F(x)$, and $R(t+1,x)=F(R(t,x))$.

\begin{theorem}[Flow law]
For all $s,t\in\mathbb N$ and $x\in S$,
\[
R(s+t,x)=R(s,R(t,x)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Iterating $F$ a total of $s+t$ times can be grouped into the first $t$ applications followed by $s$ applications. Equivalently, $F^{s+t}=F^s\circ F^t$, as follows by induction.
\end{proof}

The theorem expresses an action of the additive monoid $(\mathbb N,+)$ on $S$.

\begin{definition}[Computation in fixed time]
Let $X$ and $Y$ be input and output spaces. Given an encoder $e:X\to S$, decoder $d:S\to Y$, runtime $T\in\mathbb N$, and target $f:X\to Y$, the system computes $f$ in $T$ steps if
\[
d(R(T,e(x)))=f(x)
\]
for every $x\in X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[One-step realization]
For every function $f:X\to Y$, there exist a state space $S$, transition $F:S\to S$, encoder $e:X\to S$, and decoder $d:S\to Y$ such that
\[
d(F(e(x)))=f(x)
\]
for every $x\in X$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $S=X\sqcup Y$, the disjoint union of pending-input and completed-output states, with injections $\iota_X$ and $\iota_Y$. Define
\[
F(\iota_X(x))=\iota_Y(f(x)),\qquad F(\iota_Y(y))=\iota_Y(y).
\]
Take $e(x)=\iota_X(x)$ and define $d(\iota_X(x))=f(x)$ and $d(\iota_Y(y))=y$. Then
\[
d(F(e(x)))=d(\iota_Y(f(x)))=f(x).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Scope]
The theorem is an exact realization result, not a uniform machine-simulation theorem. The state space and all maps may depend on $f$, and the construction places $f$ directly in the transition and decoder. It supplies no bounds on circuit size, neuron count, precision, fan-in, or description complexity. A fixed-architecture simulation theorem would instead encode configurations of a standard machine and preserve its transitions, halting, and output with controlled overhead.
\end{remark}

\section{Finite-state recurrence}

Assume throughout this section that $S$ is finite. The orbit map $t\mapsto R(t,x)$ sends an infinite set into a finite one.

\begin{theorem}[Orbit collision]
For every $x\in S$, there exist $i,j\in\mathbb N$ with $i<j$ such that
\[
R(i,x)=R(j,x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If all orbit states were distinct, they would form an infinite subset of finite $S$. The pigeonhole principle supplies two distinct times with equal states; order them as $i<j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Propagation of collisions]
If $R(i,x)=R(j,x)$, then for every $k\in\mathbb N$,
\[
R(k+i,x)=R(k+j,x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Apply $R(k,\cdot)=F^k$ to both sides and use the flow law.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Periodic-tail theorem]
Every orbit on a finite state space is eventually periodic. For every $x\in S$ there exist $a,p\in\mathbb N$ with $p>0$ such that, for all $k\in\mathbb N$,
\[
R(a+k+p,x)=R(a+k,x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $R(i,x)=R(j,x)$ with $i<j$. Set $a=i$ and $p=j-i$. Then $p>0$ and $a+p=j$. Propagation of the collision through $k$ further steps gives the stated equality after rearranging addition.
\end{proof}

The functional graph of $F$ gives a geometric interpretation. Every vertex has one outgoing edge. An orbit follows a directed path; after revisiting a vertex, determinism forces it around the same cycle forever. Thus every orbit consists of a transient stem followed by a loop.

If $|S|=N$, the first $N+1$ orbit states already contain a collision. Consequently one can sharpen the result to a start $a<N$ and period $p\leq N$.

\begin{theorem}[Observed periodic tail]
Let $Z$ be any set and $h:S\to Z$ any observation. For every $x\in S$, there exist $a,p\in\mathbb N$ with $p>0$ such that, for every $k\in\mathbb N$,
\[
h(R(a+k+p,x))=h(R(a+k,x)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply $h$ to both sides of the periodic-tail equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No injective natural-valued observation]
For no observation $h:S\to\mathbb N$ is the sequence $t\mapsto h(R(t,x))$ injective.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
At the two distinct collision times $i<j$, the internal states, and therefore their observations, agree.
\end{proof}

This is a finite-model obstruction to an indefinitely novel output stream. Any proposed super-Turing mechanism must leave at least one assumption: finite state, deterministic autonomous update, recurrence-preserving observation, or isolation from external novelty. Continuous state alone is insufficient to establish hypercomputation. A real initial condition can hide a noncomputable sequence in infinitely many digits; reading it uses oracle information rather than creating it. Effective preparation, admissible constants, finite precision, robustness, and time resources must all be specified.

\section{Configuration families and information cost}

Let $[n]=\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$.

\begin{definition}[Deterministic transition configuration]
A deterministic transition configuration is a function $F:[n]\to[n]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Transition-map count]
The number of deterministic transition configurations is
\[
N_W(n)=n^n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For each of $n$ input states, the rule independently chooses one of $n$ outputs. Multiplication gives $n^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Binary connection configuration]
A binary connection configuration is a function
\[
A:[n]\times[n]\to\{0,1\},
\]
equivalently an $n\times n$ Boolean matrix. Directed and self-connections are allowed.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Binary-matrix count]
The number of binary connection configurations is
\[
N_S(n)=2^{n^2}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are $n^2$ ordered pairs and two independent choices for each corresponding entry.
\end{proof}

The families encode different freedoms. A transition map chooses one successor per current state. A matrix chooses an independent binary relation for every ordered pair.

\begin{definition}[Configuration information]
For a finite family containing $N$ distinguishable configurations, its ideal information cost is
\[
I(N)=\log_2 N
\]
bits. Define
\[
E_W(n)=\log_2 N_W(n),\qquad E_S(n)=\log_2 N_S(n).
\]
\end{definition}

When $N$ is not a power of two, an integral fixed-length code needs $\lceil\log_2N\rceil$ bits. The real-valued logarithm is the standard information quantity and gives exact asymptotic comparisons.

\begin{theorem}[Exact information formulas]
For every positive integer $n$,
\[
E_W(n)=n\log_2n,\qquad E_S(n)=n^2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the two counts and use $\log_2(a^b)=b\log_2a$ and $\log_22=1$.
\end{proof}

Thus the costs have orders $\Theta(n\log n)$ and $\Theta(n^2)$, but the equalities are stronger than asymptotic estimates.

\begin{lemma}
For every integer $n\geq1$, $\log_2n<n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The elementary inequality $n<2^n$ holds for every positive integer. Since $\log_2$ is strictly increasing,
\[
\log_2n<\log_2(2^n)=n.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict information advantage]
For every integer $n\geq1$,
\[
E_W(n)<E_S(n).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Multiply $\log_2n<n$ by $n>0$ and use the exact formulas:
\[
E_W(n)=n\log_2n<n^2=E_S(n).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing relative cost]
The ratio of deterministic-transition information to binary-connectivity information tends to zero:
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{E_W(n)}{E_S(n)}=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For $n>0$,
\[
\frac{E_W(n)}{E_S(n)}=\frac{\log_2n}{n}
=\frac{1}{\ln2}\frac{\ln n}{n}.
\]
The standard limit $(\ln x)/x\to0$ proves the claim. It follows, for example, from l'H\^{o}pital's rule.
\end{proof}

Equivalently, $E_W(n)=o(E_S(n))$: for every $\varepsilon>0$, sufficiently large $n$ satisfy $E_W(n)<\varepsilon E_S(n)$.

\subsection{Benchmarks}

At $n=2$,
\[
N_W(2)=4,\quad N_S(2)=16,\quad E_W(2)=2,\quad E_S(2)=4.
\]
At $n=4$,
\[
N_W(4)=256,\quad N_S(4)=65{,}536,\quad E_W(4)=8,\quad E_S(4)=16.
\]
For $n=2^m$,
\[
E_W(n)=mn,\qquad E_S(n)=n^2,\qquad \frac{E_W(n)}{E_S(n)}=\frac{m}{2^m}.
\]
This makes the decay explicit.

\section{Algorithms and numerical exploration}

\subsection{Energy profiles}
Given $N$, evaluate $E_W(n)$, $E_S(n)$, and their ratio for $1\leq n\leq N$. Each row takes constant-time floating-point arithmetic, giving $O(N)$ time. Streaming uses $O(1)$ auxiliary memory; retaining all rows uses $O(N)$.

\subsection{Orbit decomposition}
For a finite transition table, keep a dictionary from each state to its first visit time. Starting at $x_0$, record unseen states and advance. When a state repeats at time $t$, let $a$ be its first visit and $p=t-a$. The algorithm returns transient length $a$ and period $p$, certifying
\[
x_{a+k+p}=x_{a+k}
\]
for every $k\geq0$. On an $N$-state space, at most $N+1$ states are inspected. Hash-table time and memory are expected $O(N)$. A tortoise-and-hare variant reduces memory to $O(1)$.

\subsection{One-step realization}
For finite $X$ and $Y$ and a table for $f:X\to Y$, create tagged states $(\mathrm{in},x)$ and $(\mathrm{out},y)$. Send each input state to $(\mathrm{out},f(x))$ and each output state to itself. Construction takes $O(|X|+|Y|)$ time once the function table is available and uses $|X|+|Y|$ states.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

Finite deterministic abstractions arise in Boolean regulatory networks, synchronous neural models, finite controllers, and discretized dynamical systems. Their long-run behavior consists of attractor cycles with transient basins. Coarse observation can recur earlier than the internal state because distinct states may share one observed value.

The count $n^n$ quantifies a structural constraint: exactly one successor is selected per input. Requiring functionality sharply reduces freedom relative to an arbitrary binary relation. This compression principle is relevant to model selection and architecture design, where structural restrictions lower description complexity even if the remaining family is large.

The physical analogy has strict limits. Configuration information does not count switching events, weighted synaptic changes, communication distance, analog precision, or error correction. Actual silicon is structured rather than an arbitrary matrix, and biological systems have weighted, stochastic, spatial, and chemical degrees of freedom. A physical trajectory cost might instead take the form
\[
C(x_0,T)=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}c(x_t,x_{t+1}),
\]
where $c$ measures switching or dissipation. Such a cost differs fundamentally from the information needed to identify a transition rule.

\section{Discussion}

The model gives a three-part picture. Deterministic dynamics is representationally universal when state spaces and interfaces are unconstrained. Finite autonomous dynamics is recurrent: collision, periodicity, and observational periodicity follow from the pigeonhole principle and determinism. Finally, deterministic transition rules form a compressed configuration family with exact information $n\log_2n$, while unrestricted binary connectivity costs $n^2$ and dominates in vanishing ratio.

There is no contradiction between one-step realization and recurrence. The former implements one input--output map using a tailored construction; the latter concerns infinite temporal behavior of a fixed finite system. A system can answer every input in a finite domain correctly while all of its autonomous trajectories eventually repeat.

Nor does the information advantage establish lower physical energy use. It compares two precisely specified design spaces. Extending the conclusion to devices requires new architecture and cost assumptions.

\section{Future work}

A first extension is uniform machine simulation: choose a concrete machine, encode configurations in a fixed state family, and prove that transitions, halting, and output commute with encoding. Explicit state, runtime, precision, and fan-in bounds would turn expressive adequacy into a resource-aware theorem.

A second extension is quantitative recurrence, incorporating sharp bounds $a<N$ and $p\leq N$, then studying coarse observations and stochastic perturbations. A third replaces description information by physical transition costs, such as changed components, weighted activity, communication distance, or dissipation.

The most delicate direction concerns continuous dynamics and proposed super-Turing behavior. A meaningful separation must distinguish computation from oracle information hidden in real constants or initial conditions. It must specify effective representations, admissible parameters, finite observation precision, perturbation robustness, and time bounds. One promising program is to prove no-hypercomputation results for robust Lipschitz systems under finite-precision decoding, then identify which assumption a stronger model must violate.

\section{Conclusion}

A deterministic wetware system consists of one state space and one iterated map. Every function has a tailored one-step realization. Every finite-state orbit eventually repeats, and every observation inherits that repetition. Deterministic transition tables on $n$ states have $n^n$ configurations and information cost $n\log_2n$; arbitrary binary $n\times n$ matrices have $2^{n^2}$ configurations and cost $n^2$. The deterministic cost is strictly smaller for every $n\geq1$, and its relative cost tends to zero.

These results locate a useful boundary. Representation can make dynamics universal in an existential sense; finiteness imposes recurrence; and functional structure creates compression. A fuller theory of biological computation should preserve these distinctions while adding uniform simulation, physical resources, noise, continuity, and robustness.

